@page "/ticketing"
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Job card and ticketing software";
    ViewData["Description"] = "Job card software for IT companies, repair shops and call-out work: workshop jobs, call-outs, walk-ins, remote sessions, quotes and a billing queue.";
    ViewData["Keywords"] = "job card software, job card app, online job card, workshop job card, ticketing system, repair shop software, call-out job cards, South Africa";
}
<section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Job cards and ticketing</h1>
        <p class="lede">A job card for every job: workshop repairs, call-outs, walk-ins and remote sessions, each following a route made
            for that kind of work — booked in, done, invoiced. Every remote session opens its own job card with the time spent, and
            every finished job lands in the office's billing queue. For IT companies, repair shops, call-out businesses and anyone
            else who works on other people's things.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><partial name="_SiteCta" /></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section" style="padding-bottom:0">
    <div class="wrap">
        <figure class="shot-figure shot-wide">
            <partial name="_Shot" model="SitePages.Shots.Tickets" />
            <figcaption>A workshop job in the console: its story step by step, with only the next steps as buttons. The same ticket is in the
                web portal for the office.</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>A route for every kind of job</h2>
        <div class="facts" style="margin-top:18px">
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Workshop</h3><p>Booked in with three photos, inspection findings, repairable or not, quote, the work done written into the job, collection or delivery with photos out. A damage report when it cannot be repaired.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Call-out</h3><p>Booked, rescheduled when it has to be, the work and time recorded on site, paid there by cash or card machine — or brought back to the workshop on the same ticket.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Walk-in and remote support</h3><p>Fixed at the counter or over a session, invoiced or paid there, or linked into a bigger job for the same client.</p></div>
            <div class="card fact"><h3>Recurring tasks</h3><p>Set up a daily, weekly or monthly task once — maintenance, billing, meter readings — and its ticket opens by itself each time, with the right people on it.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:18px">Each ticket reads like a story: what happened at each step, by whom, with the next steps as buttons. Made a mistake?
            Go back a step. Several people can be on one ticket, and an @@mention stays flagged until the person answers.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap grid2">
        <div>
            <h2>Quotes to your suppliers</h2>
            <p>List the parts — with photos and serial numbers if you like — tick the suppliers, and a quote request goes out asking price,
                stock and delivery, signed with your own signature. One supplier goes in To; several go in Bcc, so nobody sees the others.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h2>The office knows what to invoice</h2>
            <p>A finished job moves to the billing queue, and the office administrators are told at once. Nothing is closed until it has been
                invoiced or marked as not to be — so nothing is forgotten.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Tickets that come to you</h2>
        <ul class="feature-list" style="margin-top:18px">
            <li>An email to your support address opens a ticket, and replies land on it</li>
            <li>A contact form for your website that opens tickets</li>
            <li>Update the client from a ticket by email — their reply comes back onto it</li>
            <li>Every session on a customer's computer is its own ticket</li>
            <li>A support request from your app arrives with a picture of their screen</li>
            <li>Import your clients from a spreadsheet you already have</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
        <h2>The whole team, one place</h2>
        <p>Owners, supervisors, technicians and office administrators each see what they need — tick the boxes a person needs, and one person
            can be several. Office chat for the team, a ding for a new message, a ring for a customer waiting. Works on a phone.</p>
        <div class="next-links"><a class="btn" href="/knowledge-base">The knowledge base</a> <a class="btn" href="/health-checks">Monthly health checks</a></div>
    </div>
</section>
